#!/usr/bin/env bash
# The one command for the Human's agent accounts (#177; its list is #173's).
#   factory [--all] [--json]       every agent profile's login, account and plan in a table, each account's quota in a second one; on the Mac mini
#                                  the rows are the teams, numbered and the accounts lettered, with 5h, 7d, reset and the rows that share each (#178)
#   factory login <account> [--no-setup]   runs `claude setup-token`, takes the token from a hidden prompt, stores it
#   factory store <account>        on the Mac mini: reads one token from stdin and stores it (what login runs over ssh)
#   factory use [<row|repo-team> [<letter>]]  on the Mac mini: the row's account in the accounts file becomes the letter's (the team's next seat
#                                  session takes it); <repo>-<team> makes the row of a team of an onboarded repository or of the factory, and that team's running
#                                  Lead alone is restarted once Herdr reports it idle (#179, #196, #311)
#   factory lead <repo> [--team <name>] [--new] [--dry-run]  starts the Lead of one team of a repository (default team: lead, #196)
#   factory lead <repo> [--team <name>] --here [-- <claude arguments>]  on the MacBook only (#349): claude in this terminal and folder as
#                                  the Lead of <repo>, with an empty GH_CONFIG_DIR, empty GH_TOKEN and GITHUB_TOKEN, the agents' git identity
#                                  (read from the Mac mini's git settings), no git credential helper, a push to GitHub over HTTPS that
#                                  fails and git over ssh off, set through --settings; what follows -- goes to claude
#   factory gh <gh arguments>      on the MacBook: gh on the Mac mini by ssh under the agents' login, in a new temporary folder there, removed after, stdin
#                                  and exit code kept; refuses gh auth but status (and its -t / --show-token), --recover, and a file given with
#                                  --body-file, --notes-file, --env-file, --input, --field or -F (pass it on stdin: --body-file -, -F name=@-,
#                                  --input -); on the Mac mini it runs gh itself (#349)
#   factory wake <repo> --issue <n> [--team <name>]  wakes the Lead of a team (#351): one line into its Claude Code inbox socket, which says to re-read
#                                  the issue (the message itself is a comment there) and types nothing into its pane; on the Mac mini it posts to the socket
#                                  its hook recorded in ~/factory-runs/<repo>-<team>/socket; on the MacBook to the socket `lead --here` recorded when that
#                                  team's Lead runs there, else the same command runs on the Mac mini by ssh; exit 0 posted, 1 not posted (with the reason), 2 usage
#   factory logout <account>       on the Mac mini: moves the account's token into ~/.agent-profiles/archive/tokens, never deleting it
# The list runs the vendors' own checks (claude auth status, codex login status, gh auth status) for the profiles of the place
# it runs in (factory when the user is FACTORY_HOST_USER, default factory, else macbook), and takes each account's quota from the run logs of its sessions. On the
# MacBook a plain run is --all (still accepted; refused on the host), and its spare Claude rows are the ~/.claude-acc<N> folders that exist. --all
# adds the factory rows and the piggery rows over ssh to the Mac mini. --json prints the rows as one JSON line.
# A row that is not logged in shows its login command; the Human runs it. The profiles are committed in lib/factory.mjs, with no
# account name; the account of a profile is in $FACTORY_STATE/accounts (default ~/.local/state/factory/accounts), one
# "<profile>=<name>" a line, local to the machine, mode 600: on the Mac mini the one source of a row's account (only `use` changes it).
# A call that takes longer than FACTORY_TIMEOUT_MS (default 15000) is shown as timed out.
# Tokens are stored at ~/.agent-profiles/tokens/<account> (mode 600, in a folder of mode 700); only the Human runs login (bin/install-factory
# puts this command on his PATH, as a symlink, which is followed here).
# On the Mac mini the table is the numbered rows (one for each team $FACTORY_STATE/accounts names, factory/<repo>-<team> of an
# onboarded repository or of `factory`, sorted; no seat row; the verifier) and the lettered accounts (every account the file
# names and every file of ~/.agent-profiles/tokens/, read by name and by mode and date only), each with its 5h, 7d, reset, the
# rows that share it, and `?` where no run of the account reported; a row whose account has no token file is red with the line to
# run. $FACTORY_STATE/accounts (<profile>=<account> a line, mode 600, written through a temporary file and a rename) is the one
# source of a row's account (#178). `factory use <row> <letter>` rewrites it (from the MacBook, `use` and `logout` run on the Mac
# mini over ssh, their arguments checked first, `ssh -t` when `use` lacks one; with an argument missing: on a terminal the list
# and a question, else the list and exit 2); the verifier's row is refused; `<repo>-<team>` in a row's place names a team of an
# onboarded repository, or of `factory`, and makes its row when there is none (`use lead-<repo>` is gone); only that team's Lead,
# if it runs (a `claude` process with `--name <repo>-<team>`, the rule `factory top` uses), is restarted, once Herdr reports it
# idle or done (its pane is in <repo>-<team>/pane): `/exit`, then `packs/pstack/bin/lead <repo> [--team <team>]` again in the same
# pane, which resumes the conversation (#179, #196, #311), and the other teams are not touched; a Lead that does not run takes the
# account at its next start, exit 0; a busy or blocked Lead, or one with no pane, keeps running and `use` says so, exit 1, and so
# does one that Claude Code's exit dialog for background work holds after `/exit` (Herdr reports it `blocked`): `use` sends Esc,
# starts nothing, and says the Lead keeps running on its old account until its next start while its new workers start on the new
# one (#315); the team's next seat session takes the new account.
# `factory logout <account>` moves tokens/<account> to ~/.agent-profiles/archive/tokens/<UTC stamp>-<account> (folder 700, by
# rename, never deleting it); its rows then start nothing. Seat 1's old seats/1/oauth-token moves to archive/seat-1/ at the next
# `login` or `use` once seat 1's account has a token.
# `factory login <account>` runs `claude setup-token` on his terminal, takes the token from a hidden prompt he pastes into
# (--no-setup skips setup-token) and stores it at ~/.agent-profiles/tokens/<account> (mode 600, folder 700): on the Mac mini
# directly, from the MacBook over ssh's stdin to `factory store <account>`, never in an argument. Only the Human runs `login`,
# `store`, `use` and `logout`: every bin/seat role and the pstack Lead deny them (#177, #178).
# `factory lead`: the team is a letter then at most 31 lowercase letters and digits, `lead` by default, and is the team of its
# claims and pull requests; on the Mac mini it runs packs/pstack/bin/lead, on the MacBook it runs that by ssh (the start is
# described in that file's header).
# `factory top`: see bin/lib/top.mjs. `factory top --line [--width N]` prints one status line and `factory top --brief [--after <seq>]`
# prints the text of Colly's start hook (#365); both exit 0 and, from the MacBook, run on the Mac mini by ssh.
src=${BASH_SOURCE[0]}
while [ -L "$src" ]; do
  dir=$(cd -P "$(dirname "$src")" && pwd)
  src=$(readlink "$src")
  case $src in /*) ;; *) src=$dir/$src ;; esac
done
# After --, node takes no argument as its own, so `--env-file` and the like reach lib/factory.mjs (gh's --env-file is one).
exec node -- "$(cd -P "$(dirname "$src")" && pwd)/lib/factory.mjs" "$@"
